00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.133
So let's get started.

00:01:02.133 --> 00:01:04.050
So one of the things
that we really believe in

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:09.097
is that organizations need cloud
native tools and endpoints.

00:01:09.097 --> 00:01:10.680
And we think this
because we've talked

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.310
to many different organizations
of all shapes and sizes that

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.630
are rapidly moving to the cloud.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:18.630
And so their employees'
experience now is really

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.310
centered on apps and devices.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.970
We see a Forester study that
showed that employers are now

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:28.060
using on average 22 cloud
based apps and three devices.

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:30.840
So these are mission critical
tools for these employees

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.170
to do their jobs.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:36.540
And we have a real opportunity
to make them better.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.040
What we found though
as we've talked

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.160
to a lot of organizations is
that managing, and scaling,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:45.210
and deploying these platforms
is actually quite tedious today.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.060
And it doesn't
scale all that well.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.950
And it's not because people are
making things over complicated.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.860
And I think when we
dig into the processes

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.020
that organizations are using,
all of them make sense.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:59.390
All of them have genuine
business reasons behind them.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.950
And nobody is trying to cut
out any step of this process.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.820
But I think the important part
is that it's not very easy

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:06.975
and it's not very scalable.

00:02:06.975 --> 00:02:08.100
And we can do a lot better.

00:02:11.727 --> 00:02:13.560
The other thing is it's
only getting harder.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:14.060
Right.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.070
So whereas at one point maybe
you were just taking a device,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.080
and imaging it, and
making sure the operating

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.240
system and the software
was up to date,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.830
and then you just send
it out into the field

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:25.290
and maybe you see
that device again

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.435
in a few months or a few years.

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:29.310
Now we're doing everything
through the cloud.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.290
And so we're thinking about
not just devices and users,

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:36.240
but also applications, also
peripherals like printers,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:40.090
network security, monitoring,
reporting, et cetera.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.320
And so there are so many
new layers to the IT puzzle

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.590
now that we have to fit
together and fit together

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.570
in a way that feels
cohesive and that

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:53.910
presents an understandable
IT policy to our employees.

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.040
Now of course, we're
adding all of these layers

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.690
at the same time
that we're adding

00:02:57.690 --> 00:03:01.330
just an unprecedented number
of devices into the mix.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:04.538
So we're making it both more
complicated and scaling it

00:03:04.538 --> 00:03:06.080
in a way that we
haven't seen before.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.630
And so I think at Google we feel
that we have the opportunity

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:16.370
to help improve the situation
by focusing on reshaping

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.230
enterprise computing.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.480
And the reason I say computing
is that on the Chrome OS team

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.910
we build the Chrome
operating system.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.790
And when I say cloud
native, what I mean

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:28.190
is that the Chrome
operating system really

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:30.320
came out of the Chrome
browser project, which

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.530
is, of course, people's first
introduction to the cloud.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.060
That's your viewport
into the cloud.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.270
But if you expand the
Chrome browser and all

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.300
of its principles, like
simplicity, and speed,

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:45.120
and security, and you turn
that into an operating system,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.850
all of a sudden
you have a device

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:49.490
that's not just
cloud ready, not just

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.010
cloud was added on top
of something existing,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.660
but actually cloud
native, meaning

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.860
it was built for that purpose.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.590
It was built with
the opportunity

00:03:57.590 --> 00:03:59.840
to do OS updates in
a cloud native way,

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.660
for instance to do policies
in the cloud native way,

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:06.050
to do management in
the cloud native way

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:09.850
as well as applications.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:11.550
So in addition to
the operating system,

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:15.420
the product that we have for
realizing this vision of cloud

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:18.329
native computing and
endpoints and tools

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:20.279
is the Google Admin console.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:23.580
And so if you're using G Suite
or you're using Mobile Device

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.440
Management, this
is the same console

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.390
that you're already
accustomed to.

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.070
And this is where
you can go to manage

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.900
every single layer
that we talked about

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.418
in that previous slide.

00:04:32.418 --> 00:04:34.710
And this is also where you
can go to manage your Chrome

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.270
devices and Chrome browsers.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.130
And so when we talk
about Chrome enterprise,

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.270
that's the umbrella
product that encompasses

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.520
all of these different things.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.830
We have fleet management.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:46.670
We've got tools.

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:49.275
We've got the
application experiences.

00:04:49.275 --> 00:04:50.900
And all of that
together is what allows

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.850
you to bring Chrome into your
enterprise in a cloud native

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:54.350
way.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.600
So I'm going to talk real
quick about fleet management.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.562
So I'll cover a few
principles that we've

00:05:02.562 --> 00:05:04.020
learned over the
years from talking

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:06.480
to many different
organizations all shapes

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.300
and sizes, all industries,
verticals, et cetera.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.140
The first is that fleet
management should be simple.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:17.180
And so what I like
everybody to picture here

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.515
is put yourself in the seat
of an airplane cockpit.

00:05:20.515 --> 00:05:21.890
And you're looking
around and you

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:25.070
see all of these hundreds or
thousands of different controls

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:27.700
and buttons and surfaces.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.700
And imagine you don't
know what most of them do.

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:30.710
Right.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.330
Would you look at
one and say, hm,

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:33.750
I wonder what that one does?

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.920
We hope that people don't
fly planes that way.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.462
But of course, that's
how a lot of us

00:05:37.462 --> 00:05:39.470
learn technology is we
just kind of experiment

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.790
and see what all
of these things do

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.030
but hopefully not so much
in an enterprise context

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:48.810
where these things can have real
consequences for our employees.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:50.810
So I want to point out
the airplane analogy,

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:52.595
because I think
you also wouldn't

00:05:52.595 --> 00:05:56.060
want to fly a plane that didn't
have those controls either.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:59.180
So simplicity is not
about taking things away,

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.310
but it's about making sure
that you don't actually

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:05.690
need to be monitoring and
modifying each of those things

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:07.170
every time.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.870
I think the realization
that we had was

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.490
that we can do things better.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:14.900
We can raise the baseline
of what organizations

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:17.630
have as their baseline
level of security,

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:20.390
and policy, and management
just because there

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.640
are a lot of commonalities
across all types

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.270
of organizations.

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:26.750
And then you can spend the
time customizing things

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:30.330
to your exact needs.

00:06:30.330 --> 00:06:33.340
Of course, simplicity is
also only getting harder.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.670
So with a cloud
native device, we

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:37.530
can now add so
many more policies

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.280
than we've ever had before.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.360
And so where as we started with
just a handful of policies,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:46.210
we now have hundreds and
hundreds of different ones.

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.820
This includes things even
down to the level of hardware,

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:51.420
so things like two
factor authentication,

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:55.770
things like TPM
firmware updates,

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.140
things like processor threading.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.660
And so I actually hope
that nobody in this crowd

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.250
has ever thought
about those things,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:04.590
because they're probably
not core drivers to what

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:06.510
your organization does.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.370
But at the same time
it's helpful to have

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:11.460
all of those things, have
all of those controls ready.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:14.010
And that's the type of area
where we can make a big impact

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:14.970
by making it simple.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.420
The other principle
that we've heard

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:27.760
is that device management
needs to be scalable.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:29.760
So with respect to this
beautiful animating gear

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:33.300
here, that actually means
more than just the raw scale

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:35.430
of the number of
devices you have.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:37.170
Of course, we're
familiar with all sorts

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:41.880
of scales of devices from just
a few to a few hundred thousand.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:45.120
But scalability also means
adapting the fleet management

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.070
tool to your type
of organization.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.500
So we work with
organizations that

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.910
have one admin who does things
part time every few Fridays

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:56.250
because that's what
the organization needs.

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:57.750
And we work with
organizations that

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:01.380
have multiple admins
with tiers of access,

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.300
different privileges
between them,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:07.770
specialized knowledge in
devices or networks or security,

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.230
needs around auditing and
compliance and making sure

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.610
all those processes work well.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.880
And those two organizations
don't necessarily

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.930
need the same
management experience.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:18.930
In fact, they probably
need different management

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:20.190
experiences.

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.950
And so scalability is not
just about making sure

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:26.310
that it goes no matter
how many devices you have

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.250
but that it adapts to the actual
needs of your organization.

00:08:33.065 --> 00:08:34.940
And lastly, our goal is
for device management

00:08:34.940 --> 00:08:36.350
to be insightful.

00:08:36.350 --> 00:08:40.010
So we really want to turn the
tables so that you're not just

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:42.260
thinking I want to
manage my devices

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:45.470
but actually my devices can
provide something back to me.

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:48.470
My devices can provide
some kind of information.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.240
So imagine that you
have a device that

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:52.610
is checking in all the
time and then suddenly it

00:08:52.610 --> 00:08:54.190
just falls off the map.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.590
You know, was that because
that device got lost or stolen,

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:59.340
or maybe the employee
is on vacation.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:08:59.840
Right.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.215
It's not necessarily
a bad thing,

00:09:01.215 --> 00:09:04.200
but it's something that might be
worth your time to investigate.

00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.230
And so insightfulness
is about providing

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.540
that kind of
proactive information

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.030
so that you can pay attention
to the right things,

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.670
because even at the scale
of a few dozen devices,

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.410
it's just impossible
to keep track

00:09:15.410 --> 00:09:17.210
all the time of every
single thing that's

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:19.067
happening in your organization.

00:09:19.067 --> 00:09:20.900
So we need to have a
tool that will actually

00:09:20.900 --> 00:09:23.480
work with you to help
make a lot of that easier.

00:09:27.187 --> 00:09:29.270
So let's get down to the
brass tacks of the things

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.720
that we're actually working on.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:33.980
The first one is
speedier administration.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.367
Next is OS update controls.

00:09:35.367 --> 00:09:36.950
We could probably
have a whole session

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:39.230
just on OS update controls.

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:42.180
We hear a lot from folks that
this is really important.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:44.180
And it's also a big
opportunity where

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:46.400
having a cloud
native device allows

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.330
us to do a lot more things.

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:50.480
It's not just about
customizing the version

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.020
but it's also about how do you
scale, and deploy, and roll out

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.580
and apply updates.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:58.250
Even OS rollback is something
that we've heard a lot about

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.000
and that we're working on now.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.352
So OS update controls are
a big area of focus for us.

00:10:04.352 --> 00:10:05.810
Reporting obviously
is something we

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:08.390
talked about with the
point about insightful.

00:10:08.390 --> 00:10:10.340
And then browser
management is one I really

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:12.050
want to draw your attention to.

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:14.720
We just announced on Tuesday
the full availability

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.690
of Chrome browser
cloud management.

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:21.810
And so if you're using Chrome
browser in your organization,

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:24.980
which if I were a betting man
I would say you probably are,

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:28.100
this is a way for you to bring
Chrome browser into the cloud

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:30.080
and actually manage it
through the same admin

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:32.800
console where you're
managing all of your devices.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.960
And so you can do things like
set policies, deploy apps

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:38.990
and extensions, manage
extensions by permission

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:41.990
if you want to, and even
get some information back

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:45.230
about what types of extensions
your users in your organization

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:46.650
are using.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:50.150
So if you're not yet on
fully cloud native devices,

00:10:50.150 --> 00:10:52.260
we understand that
that takes some time.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.900
But browser management
is a great way

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:56.720
to get started today
with having some

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:58.610
of the magic of the
cloud management

00:10:58.610 --> 00:11:00.131
in your organization.

00:11:03.488 --> 00:11:05.780
So I'm going to go through
a couple of real quick demos

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:06.377
here.

00:11:06.377 --> 00:11:08.210
One thing I want to
point out is that if you

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:10.460
are familiar with our
Google Admin console,

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:12.740
you'll notice that these
look a little bit different.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:16.190
And that's because we're working
on a full visual refresh,

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:19.443
and adding a few new features,
and making it a lot faster.

00:11:19.443 --> 00:11:21.110
So this stuff may
look a little bit new.

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:22.680
That's intentional.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.820
And we have a number of trusted
tester and beta programs

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:28.613
if you're interested
in trying it out.

00:11:28.613 --> 00:11:30.030
So for the first
quick demo, we're

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:32.790
going to do a device
search and disable.

00:11:32.790 --> 00:11:36.120
So imagine that you have
a fictional organization

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:38.250
and we've got an
employee named Sandra.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:41.320
And she's leaving
the organization.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:42.750
So what we can do
is just go ahead

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.960
and search for Sandra's name.

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.770
And we're going to find all of
her devices in the device list.

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:51.940
She has 15 of them, so she
must have been productive.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:53.940
We're going to just go
ahead and disable them.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.410
And what you might
catch at the end

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:56.910
there is that we're
actually putting

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:58.620
a message on the
device itself that

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:01.590
says property of
organization, please return

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:03.840
to this address,
that kind of thing.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:05.340
And so this is a
great example where

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:08.910
having a cloud native tool and
a cloud native device really

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.790
work together well, because
we have this just built

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.580
in to every single Chromebook
that you're managing.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.760
And when you think about things
like information security

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.420
and data lockdown and whatnot,
this is a really, really easy

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.860
and integrated way to
do all of that stuff.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:25.510
You don't need third
party software.

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.010
You don't need to
buy anything else.

00:12:27.010 --> 00:12:29.160
It's just part of the
Chrome enterprise product.

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:34.360
Next is log capture.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:35.830
So we understand
sometimes people

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:39.010
need to capture logs about
their devices for any reason.

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:42.770
And with Chrome Kiosk
devices, you can do just that.

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.030
So I'm going to go ahead
and capture logs here.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.030
This is actually going
to run in the background.

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:49.300
So if you want to go to
somewhere else in the Admin

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:51.550
console and do another
thing while it's running,

00:12:51.550 --> 00:12:52.968
you can do that.

00:12:52.968 --> 00:12:55.510
Of course, once it's done, you
can go ahead and just download

00:12:55.510 --> 00:12:56.080
the file.

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:58.570
So we'll give you a zip file
with a bunch of different logs

00:12:58.570 --> 00:12:59.780
from the device.

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:02.030
We can open up the event
log in a text editor,

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:03.970
find anything we need.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:07.078
And again, this is not the only
log capture system out there,

00:13:07.078 --> 00:13:09.370
but it shows the power of
having things that are really

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:12.610
fully integrated, having a cloud
native device with the cloud

00:13:12.610 --> 00:13:17.317
native management working
together really well.

00:13:17.317 --> 00:13:19.400
The last thing I'll mention
about fleet management

00:13:19.400 --> 00:13:21.740
is, again, that we are running
trusted tester programs

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.460
on these things all the time.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:25.587
And so if you like anything
you're seeing so far,

00:13:25.587 --> 00:13:27.920
or you'd like to try them out
and give us some feedback,

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:29.420
we'd love to have you do that.

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:31.730
Please just come see us
right outside the door

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:35.480
after this and we'll be happy
to take down your information

00:13:35.480 --> 00:13:37.545
and get you set up with that.

00:13:37.545 --> 00:13:39.920
I also want to mention if
you're using the Trusted Tester

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:42.560
program and you
send feedback to us,

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:44.540
and you're wondering
where that feedback goes,

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:46.380
it goes to my inbox.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:48.470
So we are really truly
reading every single word

00:13:48.470 --> 00:13:49.770
that you send us.

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:53.510
We care a lot about building a
tool that is actually simple,

00:13:53.510 --> 00:13:55.105
and scalable, and insightful.

00:13:55.105 --> 00:13:56.730
And we can only do
that with your help.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.820
So thank you very much for that.

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:00.290
Next I'm going to
invite Marcus up

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:04.019
onto the stage to talk about
application management.

00:14:04.019 --> 00:14:07.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.300
MARCUS KOHLER: So thanks, Max,
for that great first overview

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:12.310
of the admin console
and also thanks

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:14.630
for everybody in the
audience for having me,

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:17.600
especially after the big
event yesterday evening.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:18.200
So I'm Marcus.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:21.940
And I will give a short
overview of the Chrome OS app

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:25.360
ecosystem in the next minutes
and thereby especially focus

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:29.740
on the question, how can
we manage these apps.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:32.290
Basically, the Chrome
OS app ecosystem

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:36.760
is driven by one core idea
that's very inherent to Google.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.770
We want the open web to succeed.

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:42.790
So imagine a world where
basically all your apps

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:43.570
are on the Cloud.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.970
So all the apps you are
using for your business

00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.200
or your entertainment
are web apps.

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:49.900
In this world, you
could basically

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:52.270
walk everywhere,
use every device,

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:55.210
log into your favorite
web app, and you are done.

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:59.350
You could just be productive or
entertained wherever you like.

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:01.930
And with being
driven by this idea,

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:04.390
we have basically these
advanced technologies

00:15:04.390 --> 00:15:08.850
of Chrome OS in the center
of our app ecosystem.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:13.780
However, we are very aware
that basically, as of today,

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:18.250
it is not possible to realize
every application as a web

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:22.420
application, because not every
system capability is actually

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:25.910
a way to assert that capability.

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.390
So that's the reason why
we have these native apps

00:15:28.390 --> 00:15:33.670
on the left side, namely
Chrome Apps and Android apps.

00:15:33.670 --> 00:15:37.060
I'm sure some of you know
that Chrome Apps are already

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.690
deprecated on Windows and
Mac and that we invest more

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:44.680
and more basically into the
integration of Android apps

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:46.780
into the Chrome OS
operating system.

00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:48.730
However, both of
these native apps

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.740
have the advantages
that you basically

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:54.870
can use all system capabilities
on the Chrome OS device.

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:58.030
And on the right side, you'll
see the Linux apps here.

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:00.430
So we are very aware
that not every developer

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:03.920
and not every user for Chrome
OS device will use Linux apps.

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:06.280
So basically, Linux
apps are [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:06.280 --> 00:16:09.560
to support developers
on Chrome OS devices.

00:16:09.560 --> 00:16:11.260
So to conclude
with Chrome OS, we

00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:14.290
don't want to build
another gated ecosystem,

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:15.700
but we want the web to succeed.

00:16:15.700 --> 00:16:19.300
And we do this by native apps,
advanced web technologies,

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.920
and Linux apps on all devices.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:25.750
I want to walk through these
three pillars now step by step

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:29.320
and especially start with
the Android apps here.

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:31.630
So when I was at a
booth the last two days,

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:34.137
basically a lot of people
were coming and talking to me.

00:16:34.137 --> 00:16:36.220
And they were surprised
that actually Android apps

00:16:36.220 --> 00:16:38.290
are running on a
Chromebook OS device.

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:39.670
So yes, it's possible.

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:42.790
You can basically just go into
the place on a Chromebook OS

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:47.323
device and install nearly
every Android app you like.

00:16:47.323 --> 00:16:48.490
There are some restrictions.

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.260
Some of them require a camera
at the back of your device.

00:16:51.260 --> 00:16:52.450
So these ones don't work.

00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:55.480
But nearly everything
works basically.

00:16:55.480 --> 00:16:58.690
And if you look at basically
the app ecosystem that

00:16:58.690 --> 00:17:01.640
enables this for you,
you can, for example,

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.740
use every communication
app you like.

00:17:03.740 --> 00:17:06.220
So if you don't like
Hangouts for whatever reason,

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:10.359
you can, for example, use
Cisco Jabber on these devices.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:12.040
You can use Cisco Webex.

00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:16.160
You can use Skype basically
to connect to your colleagues.

00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:19.460
You can also use, for example,
[INAUDIBLE] system close

00:17:19.460 --> 00:17:22.119
and networking
apps like VPN apps

00:17:22.119 --> 00:17:24.280
to log in basically into
the corporate network

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:26.770
of your company.

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:30.490
For example, one of
our colleagues at F5.

00:17:30.490 --> 00:17:33.530
And you can also
use Read EI apps.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:37.990
So for example, you can use
Citrix, VMWare, Microsoft, RDP.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:40.060
All these apps
are basically part

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:41.920
of the Play Store as of today.

00:17:41.920 --> 00:17:43.780
And with these VI
apps, you can use

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:48.290
for example applications that
are very computation intensive.

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:49.960
So if you want to,
for example, design

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:54.040
for these things on your device
like using a CATIA software,

00:17:54.040 --> 00:17:57.790
it probably doesn't make sense
to carry this big notebook that

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:01.960
is needed to execute CATIA
all the time around with you.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:03.910
You can just run this
in the cloud and stream

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:06.345
it via VDI to your device.

00:18:06.345 --> 00:18:07.720
And actually, the
cooler thing is

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:09.887
that for all these Android
apps, they are coming out

00:18:09.887 --> 00:18:11.680
of the Play Store.

00:18:11.680 --> 00:18:14.280
That means that basically
the Play Store already

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:17.080
filters for harmful apps,
which helps to increase

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:18.970
the security of your device.

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:22.480
And as an admin, you can even go
one step further and whitelist

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:25.820
specific apps for your users.

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:27.580
So basically, you
prepare a set of apps

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:29.590
that your users should install.

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:31.870
And you can even
go one step further

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:34.900
and say, OK, I want to
install this and this app

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:36.610
by default for certain users.

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:41.073
So that's called force
installer in our admin console.

00:18:41.073 --> 00:18:42.490
And this allows
basically the user

00:18:42.490 --> 00:18:44.170
to use these apps
at the moment where

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:45.850
he logs in into his Chromebook.

00:18:45.850 --> 00:18:47.900
These apps are
installed instantly.

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:50.560
And you can use them
just by default.

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:52.610
We'll see this later on.

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:53.110
OK.

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:56.320
But let's move to the core
of what is basically the web

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:58.970
apps here.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.520
Can we do everything with
web apps as of today?

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:02.440
No.

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.630
If you walk around and you see
basically Gmail, Google Drive,

00:19:05.630 --> 00:19:07.380
G Suite, you can
have the impression

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:08.730
that it's already possible.

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:12.570
But the true answer is actually
it's not possible as of today,

00:19:12.570 --> 00:19:15.240
because some capabilities
are still missing.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:18.300
And to basically close the
gap between today's web apps

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:21.990
and the native apps that we
have on the system as of today,

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:23.490
we have three clear
initiatives that

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:26.530
are described on this slide.

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:27.990
For example, we
have one initiative

00:19:27.990 --> 00:19:30.570
that has the target
to basically provide

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:34.480
all APIs and capabilities
to support applications

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:34.980
in the web.

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:38.890
So this includes, for
example, system notifications.

00:19:38.890 --> 00:19:41.090
Basically every app should
be possible to erase

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:42.210
system notifications.

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:45.510
Every app should be
able to create sockets

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:47.890
or an app should be able
to access the file system.

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:50.940
So we are adding these
capabilities step by step,

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:54.330
not only on Chrome OS but in
general with the Chrome browser

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:57.210
and a lot of
platforms out there.

00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:00.090
The second step is that
you have progressive web

00:20:00.090 --> 00:20:02.970
apps, especially desktop
progressive web apps, which

00:20:02.970 --> 00:20:07.710
allow to install apps offline,
web apps offline on any device

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:08.970
that you like.

00:20:08.970 --> 00:20:11.970
And your first step is that you
can basically use Web Assembly

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:16.200
to execute your C++ code,
which helps on the one hand

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.990
to migrate apps from
one platform to another,

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:22.410
but on the other hand
speeds up web [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:24.720
So we have the
example of Figma here.

00:20:24.720 --> 00:20:26.790
So Figma is quite a
nice app basically

00:20:26.790 --> 00:20:27.915
to design Android apps.

00:20:27.915 --> 00:20:29.790
And you can run it
completely in the browser.

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:33.150
It's using web
assembly based on C++.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:35.880
If you want to know more about
these progressive web app

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.850
technologies, I
invite you to visit

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:42.480
a session later on today from
my colleague Thomas, ND203,

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:45.000
developing on Chrome.

00:20:45.000 --> 00:20:49.240
And it's at 11:40.

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:52.330
And last but not least, we have
Linux running on Chrome OS.

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:56.030
So as I said, it's
targeted for developers.

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:59.470
We have Debian running data
where you can basically

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:02.680
execute your Linux
apps and they are

00:21:02.680 --> 00:21:06.580
quite sandboxed from the other
apps on the Chrome OS device.

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:09.940
And [INAUDIBLE] has been set
up that we have running here

00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:12.400
where we see a lot of tractions,
for example that you run

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:14.690
Android Studio on a
Chromebook OS device

00:21:14.690 --> 00:21:18.490
and then you can basically test
the developed Android apps also

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:20.440
directly on this device.

00:21:20.440 --> 00:21:22.060
And that's actually well used.

00:21:22.060 --> 00:21:25.255
However, to be honest,
like the Linux integration

00:21:25.255 --> 00:21:27.130
across [INAUDIBLE] is
still at an early stage

00:21:27.130 --> 00:21:30.910
so it runs on nearly all of
the Intel based Chromebooks.

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:34.240
However, the Admin Console
Management here at the moment

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:36.650
is at a stage where it can
only turn it off or on.

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:39.130
So we are planning to add
these capabilities later

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:41.310
on this year.

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:43.538
To conclude the
app section, I want

00:21:43.538 --> 00:21:45.330
to highlight that
basically all of the apps

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.340
are kept separated
via sandboxes.

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:50.680
So you know this probably very
well from your Chrome browser.

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:53.850
So if one tab crashes, not the
whole browsers crash but only

00:21:53.850 --> 00:21:56.880
this tab is basically shut down.

00:21:56.880 --> 00:22:00.270
And basically every tab is
isolated from the other tabs

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:01.200
in your browser.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:03.210
The same holds true
for the web apps

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:06.450
for Chrome extensions for
Chrome Apps and Android

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:07.990
apps on your Chrome OS device.

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:13.318
Let's now move on basically
to the management.

00:22:13.318 --> 00:22:15.110
And if you look at
management, we basically

00:22:15.110 --> 00:22:20.570
have three main ways to
manage Chrome OS apps.

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:22.370
And let's start
with the first one.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:25.100
This is basically you allow
the user to do what you like.

00:22:25.100 --> 00:22:27.950
So basically you trust
the user to execute apps.

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.690
You don't influence it a lot.

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:32.840
And this setup is
probably preferable

00:22:32.840 --> 00:22:35.150
if you have like administrators
in your user base

00:22:35.150 --> 00:22:38.960
where you don't want to restrict
a lot because you feel OK.

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:40.576
They know what to do.

00:22:40.576 --> 00:22:42.710
I grant them all positions.

00:22:42.710 --> 00:22:45.760
Second way to manage apps is via
blacklisting and whitelisting

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:46.310
thing.

00:22:46.310 --> 00:22:48.590
So blacklisting and
whitelisting works quite nicely

00:22:48.590 --> 00:22:49.520
for web apps.

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:53.930
It works for Chrome apps,
extensions, and also

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:54.720
Android apps.

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:58.640
So the idea is that you
either exclude specific apps

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:00.170
from being installed
on the user's

00:23:00.170 --> 00:23:02.450
devices or your whitelist
them so it basically

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:07.100
say, OK, the user has the right
to install this or that app.

00:23:07.100 --> 00:23:10.880
And therefore you can basically
create a fairly good set

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:13.430
of apps and basically
restrict the user to what

00:23:13.430 --> 00:23:15.380
he can do on this device.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:17.840
And third step, I
already mentioned it,

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:20.250
is to force install
apps on the device.

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:23.390
So you can basically say this
app should run on the user's

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:26.290
device every time he logs in.

00:23:26.290 --> 00:23:27.910
And you can combine these.

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:31.400
You can whitelist certain apps
and force install other apps.

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:33.260
And what's actually
pretty cool, and what

00:23:33.260 --> 00:23:34.730
I like about the
Admin Console here

00:23:34.730 --> 00:23:37.310
is that basically
the Admin Console

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:38.750
has quite smart defaults.

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:41.210
So for example, if
you are an enterprise

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:43.970
and you start the admin
console with its defaults,

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:46.100
you will see that
Android is activated

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:48.710
by default with all apps
available to the user.

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:52.010
So basically, if you
don't do anything,

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:53.750
users are in the
position to decide

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:55.545
which Android apps
they want to install,

00:23:55.545 --> 00:23:57.170
which makes quite a
lot of sense if you

00:23:57.170 --> 00:23:58.980
look at an enterprise user.

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:00.560
However, if you
are like a school,

00:24:00.560 --> 00:24:03.110
so you have basically
an education license,

00:24:03.110 --> 00:24:06.350
the default setting for schools
is that Android is first

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:07.320
shut down.

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:09.350
And even if you
activate it, it's

00:24:09.350 --> 00:24:13.250
activated in a managed mode,
which means that you basically

00:24:13.250 --> 00:24:16.010
whitelist the apps that are
available for these students.

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:18.210
And the reasoning behind
this is quite clear.

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:21.440
We don't want to play students
games during the lessons,

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:24.780
because nobody would listen
to the teacher any longer.

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:27.415
So basically, the Admin
Console helps you quite a lot

00:24:27.415 --> 00:24:29.040
to have the right
defaults here and not

00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:33.310
to do the wrong
thing by any chance.

00:24:33.310 --> 00:24:34.360
OK.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:37.210
Let's maybe have a
look at some examples

00:24:37.210 --> 00:24:40.820
here that we brought
from the Admin Console.

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:43.720
So the first one is the
integrated app list.

00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:47.770
So all the apps that you
have in Android on Chrome OS

00:24:47.770 --> 00:24:49.962
are basically listed in
this integrated app list

00:24:49.962 --> 00:24:52.420
so you have the Chrome Apps,
Chrome extensions, and Android

00:24:52.420 --> 00:24:54.070
apps in this list.

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:57.160
And you can easily see what apps
are installed on your device.

00:24:57.160 --> 00:24:59.680
And you can easily
search for them.

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:02.830
So we will see that we basically
filter first for all Android

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:04.900
apps and then we look
for a Google Drive

00:25:04.900 --> 00:25:06.550
app in this example.

00:25:06.550 --> 00:25:08.770
So we go into this list.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:11.200
We click on Type,
click on Android,

00:25:11.200 --> 00:25:13.450
and then we have the
Android list apps,

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:15.790
or if you just type
drive there, you

00:25:15.790 --> 00:25:17.200
see basically the
drive apps that

00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:19.900
are available for user base.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:24.230
And the second example
actually is this one.

00:25:24.230 --> 00:25:26.800
So here we basically
want to add a new Play

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:28.210
app on the user's devices.

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:30.430
And we don't want
to just add it.

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:32.580
We want to force it
solid and pin it.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:34.570
Pinning means that
basically the icon

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:37.780
is shown at the lower
bar on the user's devices

00:25:37.780 --> 00:25:39.930
so that he can easily access it.

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:42.070
And if you want to
do that basically,

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:43.570
you go to the same list.

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:45.250
You click on the plus
button down there

00:25:45.250 --> 00:25:47.320
and you go to the
Play Store and then

00:25:47.320 --> 00:25:52.360
you search for the Sheets
apps inside this Play store.

00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:54.160
Click on it and click Select.

00:25:54.160 --> 00:25:57.100
And from this time on, you
will have the Sheets app

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:58.480
in your app lists.

00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:00.820
And now you can basically
select what you want to do it.

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:02.830
You can allow install
it but you can also

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:06.290
force install it, block it, pin
it, or force install and pin

00:26:06.290 --> 00:26:06.790
it.

00:26:06.790 --> 00:26:09.190
And that's what we do here.

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:10.210
All right.

00:26:10.210 --> 00:26:11.590
This already concludes my part.

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:14.650
And now I want to hand
over to Derek who gives us

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:17.720
basically a true use case.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:18.220
Yeah.

00:26:18.220 --> 00:26:21.660
Explains his example and yeah.

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:24.585
It's quite very [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:24.585 --> 00:26:27.830
DEREK MCWILLIAMS:
Thank you, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:29.250
Morning, everyone.

00:26:29.250 --> 00:26:31.640
Good to see you all so bright
and early on a Thursday

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:32.390
morning.

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:34.390
Thanks Max, and Marcus,
and Google for having me

00:26:34.390 --> 00:26:35.430
here today.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:38.990
So like all the stuff that you
saw in the slides previous,

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:42.020
I'm really excited to talk to
you about how SoulCycle used

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:43.850
basically a lot of
technology that you

00:26:43.850 --> 00:26:48.710
saw in transforming all of
our studios to Pixelbooks.

00:26:48.710 --> 00:26:50.840
Want to give you a little
bit of history first.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:53.750
So SoulCycle opened in 2006.

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:56.930
We had one studio, 30 bikes.

00:26:56.930 --> 00:26:59.610
And in the 13 years
that we've been open,

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:01.940
we're now at 92 studios.

00:27:01.940 --> 00:27:03.620
During times of
rapid growth, that

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:07.280
could mean 12 to 14
studios per year.

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:10.340
And super excited to say that
we'll be opening our first here

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:14.500
in London in I want to say June.

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:18.220
Each studio has seven to
eight classes per day.

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:21.418
And that makes about 600 rides.

00:27:21.418 --> 00:27:22.960
I'll leave you guys
to do some napkin

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:25.060
math on what that looks like.

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:28.940
And then to make all that
work from the tech side,

00:27:28.940 --> 00:27:32.440
we're looking at 2,500 staff
across the United States

00:27:32.440 --> 00:27:37.670
and Canada and 500
computers across the fleet.

00:27:37.670 --> 00:27:41.510
So when we started to look
at replacing our existing

00:27:41.510 --> 00:27:44.480
technology for
new technology, we

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:47.000
came up with a few
rules and a challenge.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:52.660
We wanted our new
technology to prepare

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:55.310
for innovation in the future.

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:57.550
So basically that means
for point of sale,

00:27:57.550 --> 00:28:01.690
running transactions day to
day, for retail transactions,

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:08.580
and also whatever is in the
looking glass of technology.

00:28:08.580 --> 00:28:11.600
We also wanted to be
able to allow our studio

00:28:11.600 --> 00:28:16.110
staff to interact with
our riders in a new way.

00:28:16.110 --> 00:28:20.270
So for any of you that have
ridden in a SoulCycle before,

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:23.960
we've got a front desk with
our studio staff behind it.

00:28:23.960 --> 00:28:27.110
And we wanted to be able to
look at what technologies could

00:28:27.110 --> 00:28:29.870
we use that we could
send our studio

00:28:29.870 --> 00:28:32.360
staff into new
areas of our studio

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:35.150
and start to look at
different types of interaction

00:28:35.150 --> 00:28:35.830
with our riders.

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:40.840
This is the interesting one.

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:45.870
What we wanted to do
was to try and find

00:28:45.870 --> 00:28:51.000
a single tool that looks after
the management of our hardware,

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:54.620
of our applications,
our users without a lot

00:28:54.620 --> 00:28:56.490
of like the
traditional glue that

00:28:56.490 --> 00:29:00.510
comes with running an enterprise
suite of hardware and end

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.660
users.

00:29:02.660 --> 00:29:07.660
Specifically, we want to look
at modern identity platform

00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:11.270
security features like
two factor authentication.

00:29:11.270 --> 00:29:16.150
We wanted to look at an easy way
for application administration

00:29:16.150 --> 00:29:19.007
and also to tie that in
together with a single hardware

00:29:19.007 --> 00:29:19.840
management platform.

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:28.070
And of course, we had a
good mix of aging computers

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:30.410
out there so we
decided how can we

00:29:30.410 --> 00:29:36.100
replace that in a quick
and simple way that

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:39.740
doesn't end up with a mix
of hardware in our studios

00:29:39.740 --> 00:29:42.540
over time.

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:44.070
Thankfully, we've
been PCI compliant

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:45.390
for several years now.

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:50.340
And in choosing our
hardware, we wanted

00:29:50.340 --> 00:29:53.680
built to make sure that we could
maintain our PCI compliance

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:55.910
but also to see
if we could reduce

00:29:55.910 --> 00:29:59.910
what that footprint would
look like over time.

00:29:59.910 --> 00:30:03.700
As with any challenge
comes a couple of rules.

00:30:03.700 --> 00:30:06.420
One, each studio
must be upgraded

00:30:06.420 --> 00:30:08.610
within two to three hours.

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:11.080
We have a nice little gap in
between our morning classes

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:12.540
and our evening classes.

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:15.480
We wanted to make sure
that we could rip out

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:19.330
the old technology, get
new technology in there,

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:22.950
but more importantly prepare the
studio staff for that new tech

00:30:22.950 --> 00:30:26.578
so that when the next
ride check in comes along

00:30:26.578 --> 00:30:28.620
they already feel comfortable
with the operations

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:29.550
that they need.

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:31.530
They already feel
comfortable with what

00:30:31.530 --> 00:30:34.080
a device may look
like both from logging

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:37.380
into the device,
using our software,

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:41.220
and primarily focusing on the
right interaction as opposed

00:30:41.220 --> 00:30:43.560
to how do I use this thing.

00:30:49.060 --> 00:30:52.890
This is probably the most
important part, making sure

00:30:52.890 --> 00:30:58.140
that our studio staff feel good
about what they're about to do.

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:02.040
Changing technology can be
really, really intimidating

00:31:02.040 --> 00:31:05.260
to people that don't
live with it every day.

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:10.020
This was one of the things
that I thought about the most.

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:12.770
OK.

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:17.760
Taking all of the challenge and
the rules into consideration,

00:31:17.760 --> 00:31:21.850
we started to look around
at a mixture of tablets,

00:31:21.850 --> 00:31:24.390
laptops, devices.

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:27.450
Do we look at replacing
our existing technology

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:30.990
with the best in
breed in that line?

00:31:34.400 --> 00:31:38.640
We've been a Gmail customer
since I want to say 2006.

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:39.530
I wasn't there.

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:46.630
We decided that the
best move forward

00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:54.040
was to leverage what our users
are already using with Gmail.

00:31:54.040 --> 00:31:57.700
We decided to look
for Chrome Enterprise

00:31:57.700 --> 00:32:01.640
and for deploying Pixelbooks
at all of our studios.

00:32:01.640 --> 00:32:04.970
With Chrome Enterprise, as you
saw in the previous slides,

00:32:04.970 --> 00:32:08.570
we're able to take a lot
of the organizational units

00:32:08.570 --> 00:32:10.310
and the policies that
we could associate

00:32:10.310 --> 00:32:14.630
with those organizational
units to identify applications

00:32:14.630 --> 00:32:18.960
to associate applications
to those specific users

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:22.910
so we could tailor their
environment or their desktop

00:32:22.910 --> 00:32:27.440
to precisely what they needed
to do to do their job the best.

00:32:27.440 --> 00:32:31.370
With Pixelbooks, we decided
to go for Pixelbooks

00:32:31.370 --> 00:32:34.160
because of, ultimately,
their form factor

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:35.480
and their aesthetic.

00:32:35.480 --> 00:32:38.410
And for those who have
Pixelbooks right now,

00:32:38.410 --> 00:32:41.420
they're pretty slick
looking devices.

00:32:41.420 --> 00:32:46.730
And also you can use them
in laptop mode, tablet mode,

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:49.062
and I'll show an example
later of how we're using them

00:32:49.062 --> 00:32:50.270
in all their different forms.

00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:58.750
In moving to Chrome
Enterprise and for Pixelbooks

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:01.580
in all of our
studios, we decided

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:04.850
to also move a lot of our
traditional file servers

00:33:04.850 --> 00:33:08.510
over to Google Drive
and Team Drive.

00:33:08.510 --> 00:33:13.330
This meant that we
would be able to unify--

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:16.390
we'd be able to get some
of our enterprise users

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:20.350
that were on more
traditional devices

00:33:20.350 --> 00:33:25.360
and increase the collaborative
function of productivity

00:33:25.360 --> 00:33:31.780
of Google Sheets,
and Notes, and Docs,

00:33:31.780 --> 00:33:33.640
and share that with
our studio staff.

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:41.790
For printing, this was
an interesting solve.

00:33:41.790 --> 00:33:44.960
So we decided to go for--

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:47.030
we looked at some
hardware based devices

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:52.370
that basically use Google Cloud
Print, which was pretty good.

00:33:52.370 --> 00:33:55.790
We ended up using a mixture
of Google Cloud Print devices,

00:33:55.790 --> 00:33:59.480
Google hybrid printers,
but also there's

00:33:59.480 --> 00:34:05.385
a Windows Cloud Connector that
we ran on our traditional print

00:34:05.385 --> 00:34:05.885
service.

00:34:11.940 --> 00:34:16.310
Thankfully, we were able to
maintain our PCI compliance.

00:34:16.310 --> 00:34:19.020
Pretty stressful thing
to actually get done.

00:34:19.020 --> 00:34:22.100
But what I wanted to
share with you is it's

00:34:22.100 --> 00:34:27.150
a couple of things that
made that happen for us.

00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:29.820
Trusted apps via the Play Store.

00:34:29.820 --> 00:34:37.190
With trusted apps we're able to
essentially prove the integrity

00:34:37.190 --> 00:34:39.920
of those applications.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:42.770
With Google asset
management, auditors

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:46.190
like to know that you
know where your stuff is,

00:34:46.190 --> 00:34:50.040
so a lot of the reporting tools
that we were able to use helped

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:52.260
in our compliance filing there.

00:34:52.260 --> 00:35:01.030
G Suite IDP using two
factor authentication

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:04.600
as part of the user
security auditors also

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:07.410
really liked, and a
couple of other things.

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:10.300
So if any of you had to
deal with PCI compliance

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:13.000
before your internal
security scans

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:15.190
where they're footprinting
the devices themselves

00:35:15.190 --> 00:35:18.150
and doing security
scans, very nice

00:35:18.150 --> 00:35:22.510
note that there were not many
open ports on a Pixelbook

00:35:22.510 --> 00:35:24.670
unlike a traditional computer
where there's usually

00:35:24.670 --> 00:35:26.950
a myriad of open ports.

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:32.240
Once we put all
that together, we

00:35:32.240 --> 00:35:34.690
sent our guys on a studio tour.

00:35:34.690 --> 00:35:38.710
So we have a fairly
small lean team.

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:42.710
Broke them out into regions.

00:35:42.710 --> 00:35:46.650
So we took California,
central, east coast.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:50.620
We were able to send one
person to each studio

00:35:50.620 --> 00:35:52.780
and easily go
through the upgrade

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:55.210
from our traditional
computer to Pixelbooks.

00:35:58.140 --> 00:35:59.960
This is our Las Vegas studio.

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:02.670
I just want to walk you through
what you're seeing right here.

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:05.520
So this is our front
desk here where

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:08.700
we've got Pixelbooks that
we use for point of sale

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:11.290
and for booking.

00:36:11.290 --> 00:36:13.000
And then you'll see
on the right hand

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:18.040
side this is a Pixelbooks
running in tablet mode

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:20.890
that we use for
digital signature

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:23.030
right of waiver application.

00:36:23.030 --> 00:36:28.700
So when a rider enters the
studio, if they're brand new,

00:36:28.700 --> 00:36:30.920
we can get them
checked in at the front

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:33.050
and then they can just
walk around the side

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:36.230
and then go sign a digital
waiver and go and ride.

00:36:36.230 --> 00:36:38.840
But also because of the
way that Pixelbooks-- now

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:41.910
that we're looking at Pixelbooks
in different iterations,

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:43.910
we can send them out to
the front of the studio,

00:36:43.910 --> 00:36:48.170
run retail, use them for
any kind of application

00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:49.310
that we see fit.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:54.430
There's a few things we
learned from all of this

00:36:54.430 --> 00:36:57.040
that I wanted to share with you.

00:36:57.040 --> 00:36:59.370
Listen to your users.

00:36:59.370 --> 00:37:03.060
I think everybody when you're
looking at replacing new tech

00:37:03.060 --> 00:37:05.430
or putting in new
tech, you think

00:37:05.430 --> 00:37:09.100
you know what your users need.

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:12.070
We spent a lot of time
sitting with people

00:37:12.070 --> 00:37:15.610
in our corporate office
and in our studios

00:37:15.610 --> 00:37:19.330
asking them questions about what
their workflows looked like.

00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:22.200
What are they doing
from day to day?

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:25.890
What problems do they have
with what they have right now?

00:37:25.890 --> 00:37:27.390
And how do we go
about fixing those?

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:32.860
In finding those
holes, be creative.

00:37:35.540 --> 00:37:36.430
I mean I've been in--

00:37:36.430 --> 00:37:40.360
I've been working in
tech now for 20 years.

00:37:40.360 --> 00:37:41.830
Sometimes there
is an expectation

00:37:41.830 --> 00:37:44.260
on how to go about
doing something.

00:37:44.260 --> 00:37:44.810
Right.

00:37:44.810 --> 00:37:45.910
I need this application.

00:37:45.910 --> 00:37:46.930
That does that.

00:37:46.930 --> 00:37:49.480
I can fix this by doing that.

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:52.180
This is an opportunity,
or what I learned

00:37:52.180 --> 00:37:56.747
was let's rethink it
again from, and looking

00:37:56.747 --> 00:37:58.330
at some of the slides
from earlier on,

00:37:58.330 --> 00:38:02.680
things like is there an Android
app that could actually solve

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:04.210
this problem a little better.

00:38:04.210 --> 00:38:06.090
Do I look at reaching out?

00:38:06.090 --> 00:38:09.880
Do I look at replacing this
app entirely with a web app?

00:38:09.880 --> 00:38:12.220
All of those things.

00:38:12.220 --> 00:38:14.400
And lastly, reach out to Google.

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:16.920
Talk to your reps.

00:38:16.920 --> 00:38:19.250
I don't think that we would
have been anywhere near as

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:21.990
successful or have been as quick
as we could have been had we

00:38:21.990 --> 00:38:24.150
not spoken to all of
our reps about how

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:28.350
do I make Team Drive
work with Pixelbooks.

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:31.050
How do I do this
